    "narrative": "For August 24, 2026, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (79.36) stands as our premier offensive anchor. Facing George Klassen (xERA 5.56), **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from the highest SP skill boost on the board (+19%), making him an elite start despite the marginal drag of an elite catcher matchup. We are also leaning heavily into the matchup against Zebby Matthews; **Lawrence Butler** (73.47) and **Zack Gelof** (61.32) are high-priority plays here. **Lawrence Butler** receives a massive +13% dynamic platoon boost, while **Zack Gelof** leverages the \"slow SP delivery\" factor\u2014crucial for our team\u2019s emphasis on stolen bases\u2014to jump into the \"Strong\" start tier. **JJ Bleday** also finds a favorable path to production, with a +14% platoon edge against Carson Whisenhunt pushing him to a 63.35 efficiency score.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Gavin Sheets**, who narrowly missed the cut at 39.23. While **Gavin Sheets** benefits from a platoon advantage against Braxton Ashcraft, his limited utility in our scarcity-focused build\u2014specifically his lack of elite sprint speed\u2014left him below the efficiency floor compared to the lineup anchors. We are proceeding with caution regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Cal Raleigh**, **Bryce Harper**, **Zack Gelof**, **Luis Arraez**, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, **Lawrence Butler**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, **JJ Bleday**, and **Justin Crawford**; should any of these players move down the order or get a surprise rest day, we will sacrifice the 5%\u20136% order-slot bonus, though their base projections remain solid. **Jonathan Aranda** remains a high-variance play to monitor, as the +5% wind-out factor is partially offset by a -12% platoon penalty, making his 55.95 score highly sensitive to actual game conditions.",
